# Review ownership.
#
# The point of this file is not gatekeeping in general — it is that a handful of paths decide whether
# the guarantee in docs/SECURITY.md §1 still holds, and a change to one of them must never be merged
# because a reviewer who was looking at something else approved the pull request it was in.
#
# Everything, by default.
*                               @pascalgross

# The guarantee's own machinery. The catalogue is closed at compile time, internal/run is the only place
# in the tree that starts a process, and the tests in internal/intent are what prove both.
/internal/intent/               @pascalgross
/internal/run/                  @pascalgross

# Local policy sovereignty. The helpers run as root and must not trust their caller; the sudoers file is
# what bounds them. A widened sudoers entry is indistinguishable from a remote shell.
/internal/policy/               @pascalgross
/internal/helper/               @pascalgross
/helpers/                       @pascalgross
/packaging/sudoers              @pascalgross
/packaging/policy.toml          @pascalgross
/packaging/farrier-agent.service @pascalgross

# Signature verification and canonical encoding. Every authorisation decision on a host is downstream of
# these two, and a subtle change here is not visible in the behaviour of anything else.
/internal/signing/              @pascalgross
/internal/canonical/            @pascalgross

# The documents that are the specification rather than a description of it.
/docs/SECURITY.md               @pascalgross
/docs/PROTOCOL.md               @pascalgross

# The supply chain. Whoever controls the release workflow and the packaging controls the agent binary on
# every host that updates from the repository — a different adversary from the one in §1, and a real one.
/.github/workflows/             @pascalgross
/packaging/                     @pascalgross
